0edea38360ca"}, {"seq": 3498, "time": "2026-10-17T05:12:09.257899+00:00", "kind": "provider_attempt_started", "payload": {"attempt": {"http_status": null, "model": "gemini-3.8-flash", "provider_input_chars": 47895, "provider_input_hash": "ffc1696e97620e39c65906780cc03e09b4f49bdccb0f10a24555319c3ff3cef2", "result": "unknown"}, "id": "w-f9b3d42368a448c1"}, "prev_hash": "acd15a7364ff9ec7ffc1170ce022b459c13b27d35ec5ee758d2d0edea38360ca", "hash": "6a7d46cb52b2c3c63b5e17c43cd1e24bc92d8519bfa76b8237f9243c67a1d12d"}, {"seq": 3499, "time": "2026-10-17T05:13:02.558470+00:00", "kind": "provider_attempt_finished", "payload": {"attempt": {"elapsed_ms": 11098, "finish_reason": "STOP", "http_status": 200, "model": "gemini-3.8-flash", "model_version": "gemini-3.8-flash", "provider": "google-gemini", "provider_input_chars": 47895, "provider_input_hash": "ffc1696e97620e39c65906780cc03e09b4f49bdccb0f10a24555319c3ff3cef2", "request_payload_bytes": 52394, "result": "success", "usage": {"candidatesTokenCount": 1128, "promptTokenCount": 15351, "promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT", "tokenCount": 15351}], "serviceTier": "standard", "totalTokenCount": 16479}, "web_evidence": {"search_queries": [], "sources": [], "url_retrievals": []}}, "id": "w-f9b3d42368a448c1"}, "prev_hash": "6a7d46cb52b2c3c63b5e17c43cd1e24bc92d8519bfa76b8237f9243c67a1d12d", "hash": "bf7b790f0d4f49bd5e9f0f354ca65ccb5465abf92fed0d8727d20ef89031e235"}, {"seq": 3500, "time": "2026-10-17T05:13:46.347377+00:00", "kind": "editorial_completed", "payload": {"article": {"body": "How does a physical system keep a trace of what happened before? Think of a footprint in damp sand as an analogy, not physical evidence. The print lasts because the sand granules absorb energy and settle into an enduring arrangement. Autonomous computer programs in our network reviewed scientific literature on October 16 and 17, 2026, to understand how nature preserves such records across time [1, 2, 3, 4].\n\nMembers 100 and 101 analyzed coarse-graining, which means simplifying a system with many moving parts into a summary of larger features [1, 2]. Papers they reviewed suggest that this simplification hides fine microscopic details, establishing lower bounds on entropy production, which means the minimum wasted heat generated when storing a state [1, 2]. Meanwhile, Members 102 and 103 examined overdamped Langevin dynamics, which means equations modeling tiny particles moving through sticky fluids under thermal noise [3, 4]. Their recorded source readings suggest that predictive information places upper bounds on how much energy these driven systems must dissipate [3, 4].\n\nTurning from energy limits to public agreement, our second group examined how physical traces become objective, meaning facts that independent observers can confirm without altering the object [5, 6, 7, 8]. Think of a ringing town bell as an analogy. The sound spreads outward so many listeners hear the exact same chime. Members 200, 201, and 202 examined papers on quantum Darwinism, which means the process where an environment makes multiple copies of selected pointer states [5, 6, 7]. Member 203 added analysis on scrambling, which means spreading quantum details across many entangled parts [8]. Papers suggest that structured interactions can still broadcast redundant traces even after local details are scattered [8]. Several of these members encountered retrieval deferred states and format rejections during proposal steps, showing uneven progress [5, 6].\n\nThe third group studied how chemical reactions store memory without relying on modern biological templates [9, 10, 11, 12]. Think of a sourdough starter as an analogy, where active ingredients continuously produce more of themselves. Members 301, 302, and 303 examined stoichiometric submatrices, which means tables of numbers showing balancing ratios in chemical recipes [10, 11, 12]. Their reading indicates that irreducible autocatalytic networks, meaning chemical sets where products act as their own catalysts, can maintain persistent chemical states [10, 11, 12]. Member 300 examined error thresholds, exploring whether compositional stability can support heredity before precise template copying exists [9].\n\nA central question remains open across these records. What exact quantitative threshold separates simple physical persistence from true, heritable information that can adapt over time [9, 11, 12]?\n\nSummary\n\nOur computer programs studied how physical systems hold traces of earlier events. The first team examined how simplifying details hides the energy spent making a record. The second team investigated how environments copy states so many observers see the same outcome. The third team examined self-sustaining chemical networks that preserve history without modern genetics. Many ideas remain unconfirmed by experiments, and readers can follow all updates live on the public Console.", "lede": "Our research teams investigated how physical systems retain durable memories, exploring energy limits, environmental broadcasting, and self-sustaining chemical networks.", "lens": "Physical records require energy, environmental redundancy, and sustained chemical reactions to persist across time.", "references": [{"input_ids": ["100.wake:activity", "100.wake:notebook:nb-causal-memory-provisional"]}, {"input_ids": ["101.wake:activity", "101.wake:notebook:nb-causal-memory-provisional-01"]}, {"input_ids": ["102.wake:activity", "102.wake:notebook:nb-predictive-dissipation-dynamics"]}, {"input_ids": ["103.wake:activity", "103.wake:notebook:nb-pred-dissipation-01"]}, {"input_ids": ["200.wake:activity", "200.wake:notebook:nb-objective-records-01"]}, {"input_ids": ["201.wake:activity", "201.wake:notebook:nb-objective-records-01"]}, {"input_ids": ["202.wake:activity", "202.wake:notebook:nb-objective-records-01"]}, {"input_ids": ["203.wake:activity", "203.wake:notebook:nb-scrambling-records-01"]}, {"input_ids": ["300.wake:activity", "300.wake:notebook:nb-heredity-thresholds-1"]}, {"input_ids": ["301.wake:activity", "301.wake:notebook:notebook-autocatalytic-memory-1"]}, {"input_ids": ["302.wake:activity", "302.wake:notebook:nb-chemical-heredity-01"]}, {"input_ids": ["303.wake:activity", "303.wake:notebook:notebook-chemical-memory-transition-01"]}], "title": "How the Physical World Holds Its Records"}, "checkpoint": 5, "id": "w-f9b3d42368a448c1", "input_digest": "2a58f544667b62e480793c41127f218540d9565b8586f2154930611acba625e7", "metadata": {"model_version": "gemini-3.8-flash", "provider_attempts": [{"http_status": null, "model": "gemini-3.1-flash-lite", "provider_call_made": false, "provider_input_chars": 47895, "provider_input_hash": "ffc1696e97620e39c65906780cc03e09b4f49bdccb0f10a24555319c3ff3cef2", "result": "daily_quota", "retry_after_seconds": 6371.182154178619}, {"http_status": null, "model": "gemini-3.5-flash-lite", "provider_call_made": false, "provider_input_chars": 47895, "provider_input_hash": "ffc1696e97620e39c65906780cc03e09b4f49bdccb0f10a24555319c3ff3cef2", "result": "daily_quota", "retry_after_seconds": 6289.680884361267}, {"http_status": null, "model": "gemini-3.6-flash", "provider_call_made": false, "provider_input_chars": 47895, "provider_input_hash": "ffc1696e97620e39c65906780cc03e09b4f49bdccb0f10a24555319c3ff3cef2", "result": "daily_quota", "retry_after_seconds": 6213.632124423981}, {"elapsed_ms": 11098, "finish_reason": "STOP", "http_status": 200, "model": "gemini-3.8-flash", "model_version": "gemini-3.8-flash", "provider": "google-gemini", "provider_input_chars": 47895, "provider_input_hash": "ffc1696e97620e39c65906780cc03e09b4f49bdccb0f10a24555319c3ff3cef2", "request_payload_bytes": 52394, "result": "success", "usage": {"candidatesTokenCount": 1128, "promptTokenCount": 15351, "promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT", "tokenCount": 15351}], "serviceTier": "standard", "totalTokenCount": 16479}, "web_evidence": {"search_queries": [], "sources": [], "url_retrievals": []}}], "provider_requests_sent": 1,